:: D05_Phone_Social [nobr]
<<HUD>>

<div class="prose-block">

<p>I open Instagram. Same feed as always. Same people from campus I haven't talked to since dropping out. Same brunch photos and gym selfies and sunset shots that all look identical.</p>

<p>But tonight I'm looking differently. I'm looking at the women. Not their bodies, not like that, but their presentation. How they pose. Which angles they use. The way they hold a coffee cup so their nails show. The tilt of a chin. The placement of hair.</p>

<p>I'm studying. I don't call it that, not even to myself. I'm scrolling, that's all, the way everyone scrolls. But my eyes linger on the outfit posts. The before-and-after skincare transformations. The tutorials where someone turns a plain face into something polished and glowing with nothing but concealer and confidence.</p>

<p>Three months ago Marcus would have scrolled past all of this without registering it. He existed in a different feed, a different algorithm, a different internet. The internet for men is sports and memes and rage bait. The internet for women is a masterclass in self-presentation disguised as entertainment.</p>

<p>I'm learning. Unconsciously, involuntarily, in the way you learn a language by listening to it spoken around you. Not fluent yet. Not even conversational. But the words are starting to stick.</p>

<p>I double-tap a photo of a girl in a sundress standing in front of a mural. She has good style. The kind of style that looks effortless but probably took thirty minutes and six outfit changes. I want to learn how to do that. I want to stand in front of something beautiful and look like I belong there.</p>

<p>The thought startles me. Not because it's new. Because it isn't. Because some version of this thought has been sitting in my head since Day 2, growing quietly like a vine I didn't plant.</p>

<p>I close the app. Plug in my phone. Roll over.</p>

</div>

<<set $d05SocialBrowsed to true>>
<<run window.statChange("fem", 1)>>

<div class="choice-block">
	<<link '<span>📱 Back to the phone</span>' "D05_Phone_Hub">><</link>>
</div>


:: D05_Phone_Selfie [nobr]
<<HUD>>

<<ImagePlaceholder "The first selfie, by Mia, for Mia" "portrait" "assets/characters/mc-female-phase1/d05-first-selfie.png">>

<div class="prose-block">

<p>I hold the phone up. The front camera shows me my own face against the ceiling.</p>

<p>I take a photo. Look at it. Delete it. Take another. The angle's wrong. Another. Too close. Another. There.</p>

<p>This one is good. Not posed, not filtered, just me looking at the camera with an expression that's somewhere between tired and curious. My hair is down now, the ponytail taken out, and it falls in loose waves that weren't there when it was short. The light from the bedside lamp is warm. My eyes look big.</p>

<p>I look like someone I'd want to know.</p>

<p>I save it. Don't post it. Don't send it. Just save it. Evidence. This is what I look like at the end of Day 5. When I look back at this photo in a week or a month or a year, I'll remember this specific version of myself. The one who had just worked her first shift and drunk her first latte and been asked to a party by a stoned kid named Kevin.</p>

<p>The camera rolls into my gallery. One photo. The first photo of Mia, by Mia, for Mia.</p>

</div>

<<set $d05SelfieTaken to true>>
<<run window.rbAddMilestone("Took the first selfie, for nobody.")>>

<div class="choice-block">
	<<link '<span>📱 Back to the phone</span>' "D05_Phone_Hub">><</link>>
</div>


:: D05_SelfPleasure [nobr]
<<HUD>>

<<ImagePlaceholder "Alone in bed, the day sitting in her body" "portrait" "assets/characters/mc-female-phase1/d05-self-pleasure.png">>

<div class="prose-block">

<p>The day sits in my body like sediment. Every interaction, every glance, every moment of being perceived collects somewhere below my navel and hums.</p>

<p>Trent watching me walk away. Kevin calling me cute. The bus stop man with his sideways glances. River's eyes when I said I was carrying something heavy. <<if $d04JordanTexted>>Jordan's texts<<else>>the whole day of being looked at<</if>>. All of it pooled in one place, warm and insistent.</p>

<p>I lie on my bed. My hand finds the waistband of my sweats without my brain giving permission. Familiar now. Not clinical the way it was on Day 2, not exploratory. My fingers know the route. They know the pressure and the pace and the small circle on the left side that sends voltage up my spine.</p>

<p>I close my eyes.</p>

<p>The images come. Trent's arms in that tank top. The way he leaned in the doorframe. Confident. Hungry. Both of them. Him and Nina. What would that even look like. Three bodies. His hands on my hips. Her mouth on my neck. The thought sends a pulse through me so sharp I gasp and my hips roll upward into my own hand.</p>

<p>Or River. Quieter. His hands, the ones that hold a coffee cup so carefully. Those hands on my waist. In my hair. His mouth tasting like espresso and chocolate. Slow. Patient. The opposite of Trent. Taking his time until I can't take mine.</p>

<p>Or nobody. Just sensation. Circles and pressure and the slick heat that builds from warm to unbearable. My fingers slide inside, two of them, curled forward against the spot that makes my thighs clench. My thumb stays on the clit. I rock against my own hand, slow then faster, and the sounds that come out of me are sounds Mia makes that Marcus never could. High, breathy, desperate. "Ahh... mmn... fuck..."</p>

<p>I come with my face in the pillow and my back arched and the orgasm rolls through me in contractions I count without meaning to. Seven. Eight. Each one sends a wave from my center to my fingertips and my toes curl and my mouth opens and the last one is so deep it feels like it starts in my spine.</p>

<p>After. Lying there. Hand still between my legs, cupped over the warmth. The dampness. The pulse that keeps going, little aftershocks, for a full minute after the main event.</p>

<p>I'm getting used to this body. More than used to it. I'm starting to enjoy it. Not in a grateful way, not "at least the orgasms are good." In an actual, unqualified, I-want-to-feel-this-again way. My body is not a consolation prize. My body is the venue.</p>

<p>I clean up. Pull my sweats back up.</p>


</div>
<<if $d04JordanTexted>>
<<Dialogue "Jordan" "😎" "goodnight gorgeous 😘" "">>
<div class="prose-block">

<p>I type "goodnight" and add a 😊 emoji and stare at the emoji for thirty seconds weighing its implications before hitting send.</p>

</div>
<</if>>
<div class="prose-block">

<p>Then I pull the blanket up and let sleep come.</p>

</div>

<<set $d05SelfPleasure to true>>
<<set $player.arousal to 0>>
<<run window.statChange("stress", -5)>>

<div class="choice-block">
	<<link '<span>🛏️ Back to my room</span>' "Home_Bedroom">><</link>>
</div>


:: D05_Sage_Flyer [nobr]
<<HUD>>

<div class="prose-block">

<p>I unfold the lavender flyer on my kitchen counter. SUNRISE YOGA. The schedule is simple. Morning flow at 7 AM, Monday through Friday. Sunset yin at 6 PM, Tuesday and Thursday. Drop-ins welcome. First class free.</p>

<p>There's a small photo of Sage in a pose that shouldn't be physically possible, one leg behind her head while the other supports her entire body weight. She looks like she's enjoying it. Below the photo, a quote in italic.</p>

<p><em>"Your body is not a problem to solve. It's a place to live."</em></p>

<p>I read that three times. The words hit differently when you're standing in a body you've had for five days. A body that still surprises me every morning in the mirror. A body I'm learning to navigate like a rental car with controls in unfamiliar places.</p>

<p>Not a problem to solve. A place to live.</p>

<p>I stick the flyer to the fridge with a magnet shaped like a cactus that was here when I moved in. Maybe next week. Maybe the morning flow. Maybe I'll walk in and Sage will beam at me and tell me my chakras are misaligned and I'll have to pretend I know what that means.</p>

<p>But the flyer stays on the fridge.</p>

</div>

<<set $sageFlyerFridge to true>>

<div class="choice-block">
	<<link '<span>🛏️ Back to my room</span>' "Home_Bedroom">><</link>>
</div>

